NNAAMMEE
       postscreen - Postfix zombie blocker

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppoossttssccrreeeenn [generic Postfix daemon options]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The Postfix ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) server provides additional protection against
       mail  server  overload.  One ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) process handles multiple in‐
       bound SMTP connections, and decides which clients may talk to a Postfix
       SMTP server process.  By keeping spambots  away,  ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8)  leaves
       more SMTP server processes available for legitimate clients, and delays
       the onset of server overload conditions.

       This  program  should  not be used on SMTP ports that receive mail from
       end-user clients (MUAs). In a typical deployment, ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) handles
       the MX service on TCP port 25, and ssmmttppdd(8) receives mail from MUAs  on
       the ssuubbmmiissssiioonn service (TCP port 587) which requires client authentica‐
       tion.   Alternatively, a site could set up a dedicated, non-postscreen,
       "port 25" server that provides ssuubbmmiissssiioonn service and client  authenti‐
       cation, but no MX service.

       ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8)  maintains  a  temporary  allowlist for clients that have
       passed a number of tests.  When  an  SMTP  client  IP  address  is  al‐
       lowlisted,  ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8)  hands  off  the  connection immediately to a
       Postfix SMTP server process. This minimizes the overhead for legitimate
       mail.

       By default, ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) logs statistics and hands off each connection
       to a Postfix SMTP server process, while excluding clients in mynetworks
       from all tests (primarily, to avoid problems with non-standard SMTP im‐
       plementations in network appliances).   This  default  mode  blocks  no
       clients, and is useful for non-destructive testing.

       In  a typical production setting, ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) is configured to reject
       mail from clients that fail one or more tests. ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8)  logs  re‐
       jected  mail with the client address, helo, sender and recipient infor‐
       mation.

       ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) is not an SMTP proxy; this is intentional.   The  purpose
       is to keep spambots away from Postfix SMTP server processes, while min‐
       imizing overhead for legitimate traffic.

SSEECCUURRIITTYY
       The ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) server is moderately security-sensitive.  It talks to
       untrusted  clients  on  the network. The process can be run chrooted at
       fixed low privilege.

